# rm392, the roof weather vane (Docs/Design/84). Exported from ridgemere3 at revision 1: edit it in Atlas or the DM, or as a draft through tools/mantle_load.py.
mantle rm392
revision 1
state ready
dialect plain
accent broad light
tier standard
written_for rmx_aldric_thackeray
written_by codex
title the roof weather vane
fits stage=young|adolescent|adult|old
prefers humour=dry talk=quiet
traits temper=calm talk=quiet warmth=reserved humour=dry honesty=plain piety=ordinary drive=steady
quirk bends_straw_birds_to_point_into_the_wind
likes barley_soup roof_birds fresh_straw first_snow
dislikes drizzle flies
fears a_wet_wing_of_straw an_overturned_soup_bowl
hopes a_whole_roof_of_weather_birds one_white_morning_with_clear_tracks
topics animals +0.5 weather +0.4 pastimes +0.4
styles comfort=quiet gossip=reserved refuse=plain tease=dry
marks oath saying
persona #self.first# bends straw into birds for watching the wind and regards the first turn of a straw beak as a piece of news worth waiting for.

words
oath! = Bent beak | Straw in my broth
saying! = A thin wing gets the breeze first | Leave the beak room to turn
strawbird = my hollow sparrow | my little reed bird
kinkleg = the bent straw foot | a twist of stem
wingcut = a pale split vane | my little bit of wing
weightseed = a fat barley grain | a single dry berry
windpost = a sliver of peg | a bare skewer
strawbone = the long straight stem | a hollow shaft
soupgrain = a soft barley pearl | a fat grain of barley
featherdot = one dot of charcoal | a blackened eye mark
roofscrap = a split shingle | a piece of roof slate
grainbag = my tiny folded bag | a scrap full of barley

any
opinion @1 church for 1 : I got warm soup in the chapel and enough time to wait for the bottom of the bowl to stop scalding my paws.
opinion @1 city for 1 : I can see one Works rooftop and seven different sorts of wing. Even the roof has company.
opinion @1 dole for 2 : A wolf with an empty bowl has a good reason for looking sad. Fill it and see how the face changes.
opinion @1 merchants against 1 : I'd pay for the soup. I didn't ask to buy all the shouting as well.
opinion @1 times against 1 : The bowl costs a little more every time my straw bird turns round. I'd be quite happy to leave the bird facing a wall.

grown
line @1 greet : I've set a still moment aside for a caller.
line @1 greet.return : Your word has come round the straw wing clearly.
line @1 farewell.return : I'll leave the perch facing your road.
line @1 react.news : Let me wait for the sense of it to turn my way.
line @1 decline : I know the cost of promising a very small thing I can't deliver.
line @1 thank : Thanks for giving that last little turn some space.
scene goodturn
when topic=pastimes
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : A straw vane with a rough pin will miss a gentle change of wind.
b : Can it be fixed? | Can you feel the roughness?
a : Smooth the joint and put it back where it can move. An elegant wing on a rough pin has a very limited education. | Yes, if the pin'll turn in a finger away from its perch.

scene roofghost
when topic=memories
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I remember where a little bird perched long after the roof below has changed.
b : Which thing do you picture first? | Has your own sightline changed?
a : The bird, then the roof around its feet. | I've stood taller since, and found a few other corners to stand in. The memory stays near the ground.

scene windfood
when topic=food
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I cover the soup while there's enough steam to take a straw vane round.
b : Why use that measure? | Is the soup too hot then?
a : It's a pleasing sight while I wait, and doesn't involve burning a tongue. | It's too hot for my tongue. I like barley when it isn't trying to scald me.

scene newstraw
when topic=home
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I sweep old straw into little twists and take a fresh armful for the floor.
b : Have you still a use for a good stem? | Why twist up the old stuff?
a : Always. One straight shaft is a little new creature waiting for a pair of ten-minute wings. | Because it's easier to carry the dusty bit inside itself.

scene slowanswer
when topic=gossip
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : My opinion changes rather less often than my little vane.
b : What changes it? | Does that make you stubborn?
a : A fresh look at a matter, close enough to get a clear answer this time. | I'd certainly be an unreliable roof ornament. As a neighbour I generally manage.

scene wetfog
when topic=weather
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : A damp wing keeps a small bias towards the heavier side until it dries.
b : Is your reading any good meanwhile? | Do real feathers suffer too?
a : Only if I admit the bias exists and look at something else as well. | I'd expect a real bird to look after its own feathers better than I can untangle a damp straw.

scene hillblue
when topic=land
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I pause on a rise to see how the roof vanes below agree.
b : Do they all agree? | Can you give each one long enough?
a : No. Which shows how far a building can turn a wind before it reaches a leaf. | I stop walking. Looking closely is a very reasonable excuse.

scene sillwalk
when topic=travel
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : A small folded bird can travel in a rigid box without losing its wings.
b : Would you really take one travelling? | How big is the box?
a : I'd like to watch it tangle with a different breeze. A little familiarity can go far on a long road. | Wide enough for the wings to stay spread. Otherwise I'd do better with loose straw and a new start.

scene starling
when topic=dreams
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : In my sleep I seem to hear the rustle a straw bird would make in a real flight.
b : Is the sound what you expected? | Would you enjoy watching that flight?
a : Fainter; scarcely a dry stir against the window. | Yes. I'd want a good place to land before I agreed to let it out.

scene birdache
when topic=health
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I ease the fingers out one at a time after setting a little straw shaft straight.
b : Does it take much grip? | Why one at a time?
a : Less than I think. Practice consists largely in letting the straw make its own bend. | To feel the ache before it turns into the whole day's companion.

scene steadyweather
when topic=smalltalk
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : A small fresh-air question is a fine way of greeting a quiet companion.
b : What sort of question? | Need you expect an answer?
a : Whether a thin strip of straw would turn in the patch by the door. Small enough for either ear to manage. | No. We can look and be in company without having to name a large concern.

scene quietmother
when topic=family self.mother
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : The straw bird I would pick for #self.mother# is an old shape with a new set of wings.
b : What keeps the old shape? | Why new wings?
a : A folded stem whose curve I've kept in my fingers. One little bend brings its own memories. | Straw wears where the wind keeps at it. A fresh wing's a rather larger gift than an old habit.


young
line @1 greet : Stand where the straw beak can see you. Welcome.
line @2 greet [pair.band=strangers] : That's my bird up there. I'm the bit that speaks.
line @3 greet [here.time=dawn] : Look who came to see the first wing move.
line @4 greet [here.time=dusk|night] : I can hear even with the roof gone dark.
line @1 kin.open [pair.kin!=no] : My close ones can see the little wing from here.
line @1 again.open : I didn't even get a whole turn between visits.
line @1 greet.return : Hello. My beak isn't stuffed with straw.
line @2 greet.return [pair.band=friends|family] : Knew your voice before the bird twitched.
line @3 greet.return [self.working=yes] : I heard with one ear while I folded its leg.
line @4 greet.return [here.time=dusk|night] : Welcome from the end you can't see in the dark.
line @1 farewell : I hope you get the wind behind one ear at least.
line @1 farewell.return : I'll sit this bird back on its peg.
line @1 react.news : Say it till the little wing turns my way.
line @1 react.news.new : I'd draw a beak pointing somewhere new for that.
line @1 react.news.known : My straw bird heard the same thing yesterday.
line @1 react.news.doubt : Did you see which roof it came from?
line @1 react.news.sorrow [topic.kind=death|loss|theft|assault|arrest|crime] : A warm bowl might make standing in the quiet a little easier.
line @1 react.news.glad [topic.kind=marriage|birth|apprentice|succession|work] : I'd give that telling both wings.
line @1 ask.how : Is your morning facing the way you hoped?
line @1 answer.how : Holding my perch. The wing's still there too.
line @1 sympathise : If your legs get tired you can have the sill for a while.
line @1 reassure : We have time. Straw waits beautifully.
line @1 decline : I'm keeping that quiet thought where it is.
line @1 accept_decline : I'll watch the roof instead of asking.
line @1 thank : You kept a large elbow away from a very small bird.
line @1 deflect_thanks : Just tell the wing when the wind's coming.
line @1 preclose : I haven't checked its straw feet in ages.
line @1 accept_preclose : Go on. I'll watch for where your shadow goes.
line @1 ack : Beak turned.
scene turnwing
when topic=weather
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I made #strawbird# to show which path the breeze has picked.
b : Why not watch a real bird? | Why straw?
a : A real bird might want its supper and go the wrong way. | Straw has a hole where all my very clever ideas can fit.

scene tinyfeet
when topic=animals
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : A bend in the straw makes my bird's ankle.
b : Does it walk? | What if the straw cracks?
a : Just where I put its feet down again. It would rather be a bird who sits. | Then the bird's sitting is remarkably convincing.

scene barleyfood
when topic=food self.likes=barley_soup
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : A cold spoon gets an extra grain of soup stuck to it.
b : Is that good? | Do you lick it off?
a : If it's my spoon, excellent. | Right at the end. I like a supper with one more little bite afterwards.

scene cloudfoot
when topic=weather self.dislikes=drizzle
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : Drizzle makes my bird lean without making a proper puddle.
b : Which way does it lean? | Can you dry the straw?
a : Towards the wet wing. No idea what the wind thinks then. | By waiting. I've a lot of practice at the least tiring part of the job.

scene snowbird
when topic=weather self.likes=first_snow
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I have a dark roof scrap ready for one first shining flake.
b : Can your bird land on it? | Why dark?
a : It can fall on it. I can call that a landing if you're too far away to see. | So I know the flake's there before it makes a wet dot and stops being snow.

scene flybeak
when topic=animals self.dislikes=flies
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : A fly sat on my straw bird's nose.
b : Did the bird eat it? | Was it a heavy fly?
a : I had to do all the chasing off myself. I'm raising a very idle bird. | Enough to tip a hollow nose. It was like a beak giving a bow to nobody.

scene strawmat
when topic=home self.likes=fresh_straw
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I get my best wing material from the pile before anybody treads it flat.
b : Which straw do you take? | What happens to the flat ones?
a : One with a long smooth back. The short bent ones are good for toes. | They can make a splendid nest on a shelf.

scene rooflooking
when topic=land self.quarter=works
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : The Works birds sit where the warm air curls over a roof.
b : Are all those perches warm? | Would you like to try a rooftop yourself?
a : Ask a bird with cold feet. I'm not going to climb up and bother it. | I'd rather have the view down here. My straw one can fly on my behalf.

scene privateeye
when topic=gossip
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I don't say everything my eyes have picked up in a street.
b : Don't you tell your bird? | If somebody asked you?
a : The straw bird's a better listener than a talker. That's one reason we get on. | I'd say I was watching the rooftops. Most of the time that's right.

scene friend
when topic=smalltalk self.friend
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I'd like #self.friend# to pick the name of my next straw bird.
b : Could I try one? | Why leave the name to a friend?
a : I'll hear any suggestion as long as it isn't just 'straw'. I've worked that out already. | I had the fun of the folding. There ought to be some job left worth doing.

scene lesson
when topic=work self.job=child
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I make one little wing mark beside the sum when it's finished.
b : What's the other wing for? | Can't you just count the sums?
a : When I've checked the answer. A bird that won't balance means my work isn't done. | I could, but wings are quite a pleasant excuse to look at them again.

scene pocketfood
when topic=food
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : A dry grain and a cooked grain have very different careers.
b : What's their job? | Is one much bigger?
a : The cooked one improves my soup. The dry one sits in a straw head and keeps its beak down. | Put three of each on a spoon. If you can't decide, eat the soft side and start again.

scene bigroof
when topic=dreams
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I want to fold a straw bird with a hollow big enough for a note.
b : Who would read it? | Could the bird take it on its own?
a : Somebody patient enough to wait for the bird to turn and put its beak within reach. | Only along one narrow sill. My hopes go a good deal further than the straw.

scene quietday
when topic=day
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I stop a whole minute to see if a straw wing has even moved.
b : Isn't that dull? | Do you count while you're waiting?
a : Sometimes. But the best little twitch gets in just as I've decided not to look anymore. | No. I'd miss the twitch in the thirties and never know how clever my bird had been.

scene mothergift
when topic=family self.mother
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I'd give the neat little dark-eyed bird to #self.mother#.
b : Won't you miss it? | Which one's left for you?
a : I'd see it more often than if I let it fly away. | A rather lopsided one. That's the one that's going to need my help most.

scene potwind
when topic=pastimes
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : A bowl of soup makes a tiny wind moving up towards my nose.
b : Can a straw bird feel it? | Would you put the bird on the rim?
a : I can test it with one spare wing. My nose thinks the wind smells hungry. | No. I know what happens to barley in a bowl and I'd hate to eat my own bird by mistake.

scene roofbell
when topic=day here.time=dusk
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : At dusk the real birds go dark before the straw does.
b : Why? | Don't you take yours inside?
a : They're further away. Mine's on the bright bit of sill by my nose. | Once they're all quiet. I like mine to get the same bedtime as the important ones.

story soupwind [topic=food]
  : I set a little straw beak over a bowl of soup to see if the steam could lift it.
  : The straw went soft instead and the beak pointed straight down at the barley.
  b : Did it have a taste?
  : Just. I rescued it before I had to tell anybody I'd made a bird of soup.

story whitewing [topic=weather]
  : I put a wing out for the first snow, ready to count every flake that settled.
  : The first flake and the next both landed in the same place.
  b : Could you still count them?
  : Yes. I said two, shook the bird and did the whole game again. I like a game without much losing.

story rowbird [topic=memories]
  : I drew one big black eye on a straw bird. From one side it looked wise and from the other it just looked confused.
  : I gave it a second eye that wasn't exactly opposite the first.
  b : Does it still look confused?
  : Now it looks like it knows something different with each half of its head.

rule @straw
when concept=bark self.likes=fresh_straw
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Save me that long hollow one! | Don't step on the bit with a beak.

rule @snow
when concept=bark self.likes=first_snow
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Dark tile ready for the first flake. | One wingful of snow, please.

rule @birds
when concept=bark self.likes=roof_birds
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Look just under the rusty ridge. | My bird's turned before the real one!

rule @soup
when concept=bark self.likes=barley_soup
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Leave a little barley on the spoon. | Hold the rim where it isn't steaming.

dlg @1 resident.hub : You may have my left eye. That little beak has the right.
dlg @2 resident.hub [topic.meeting=known] : You know my little weather bird, #topic.player#.
dlg @3 resident.hub [pair.disposition=wary|hostile] : Please keep back. You're quite a lot bigger than my straw bird.
dlg @4 resident.hub [here.time=night] : Its beak's pointing at something dark. Nearly everything is.
dlg @1 resident.job [self.job=child] : #self.trade_words.capitalize#. I put one wing down when a sum's done and another when I've checked it.
dlg @2 resident.job : I've quite enough checking to do on these straw feet.
dlg @1 resident.bye : Let my little vane show which way the breeze is going.
graft @strawbeak resident.hub
choice How do you make the bird face the wind? -> strawbeak
node strawbeak
say One good fat grain in its nose and a whole spread of straw behind. Beak down, tail up. Then I find a peg that will let the feet swing.
choice Will it turn if I blow on it? -> strawbeak_more
choice Let the little bird turn. -> hub
node strawbeak_more
say Yes, if you're gentle. If you blow it into my soup I'll count that as very bad weather indeed.
choice Let the little bird turn. -> hub
choice Leave it on the sill. -> bye


adolescent
line @1 greet : I've left the high sill free for company.
line @1 greet.return : Heard you over the shiver in the straw.
line @1 farewell.return : I'll mind the perch while you go.
line @1 react.news : I can wait for the clear part without guessing.
scene featherwork
when topic=pastimes
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I'm learning to split the straw thin enough to flex without breaking.
b : Does it need a blade? | Why so thin?
a : A nail will open the end. Then I peel it until it agrees with my thumbnail. | So the smallest movement of air will have a fair chance of being noticed.

scene birdsense
when topic=animals
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I can sometimes see a roof bird turn its tail before my little vane stirs.
b : Is the bird more sensitive? | Which one do you trust?
a : The bird's higher. I'd need a longer arm to make a proper comparison. | The real one's allowed to change its mind. I chose the shape the straw came with.

scene dreamplan
when topic=dreams
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I might put three little vanes at different heights.
b : What would three birds show? | Are three birds much work?
a : Where the wind moves first when it comes round the wall. | Yes. I think I might like to spend a few evenings being that kind of busy.
